// jev-bench labelled set. One JSON object per line; lines starting with // are ignored.
// Fields: id, tier (A real / B constructed), source, claim, section, label, provenance, why, contested?
// Tier A claims are verbatim sentences from content/drafts/2026-09-18-self-improving-ai-degrades/
// and the labels come from the nine independent source audits filed beside that article.
// provenance "verbatim" = the section text is quoted verbatim from the paper in the audit.
// provenance "audit-reported" = the section is assembled from what the audit reports the paper says.
{"id": "sb-01", "tier": "A", "source": "SkillsBench v1 (arXiv:2602.12670v1)", "claim": "SkillsBench (arXiv 2602.12670, a large multi-institution author list including Dawn Song, 87 tasks across 8 domains) is the cleanest third-party result here, because it tests systems it didn't build.", "section": "From the version 1 paper: the abstract describes \"a benchmark of 86 tasks across 11 domains\". Section 2.5 says the evaluation uses 84 tasks across 11 domains, two having been excluded, one for a GPU requirement and one for verifier timeout. The first-page author block lists 40 named authors and 23 affiliation markers, among them BenchFlow, Amazon, Ohio State, Dartmouth, Stanford, UC Davis, Carnegie Mellon and UC Berkeley. Dawn Song is not among them. The paper reports that 105 academic and industry contributors submitted 322 candidate tasks, that the authors ran human review, and that benchmark Skills were selected from the top quality quartile.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "audit-reported", "why": "The task and domain counts and the named author come from the current version 4 abstract, not the version 1 paper cited. v1 says 86 across 11, 84 evaluated, and does not list Dawn Song."}
{"id": "sb-02", "tier": "A", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1 Finding 1 and Appendix A", "claim": "Skills written by humans lifted average pass rate by 16.2 points across seven model and harness configurations.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 1: \"Skills improve performance by +16.2pp on average across 7 model-harness configurations, but with high variance across configurations (range: +13.6pp to +23.3pp).\" The paper names this experimental condition \"curated Skills\" throughout. Appendix A reports that the Skill ecosystem was collected from public GitHub repositories, community marketplaces and corporate partner contributions, after which the authors selected high-quality benchmark Skills. The paper requires that task instructions be authored by contributors and reviewed; it makes no equivalent statement about the authorship of every evaluated Skill.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The figure and the configuration count are right. \"Written by humans\" is not what the source establishes: the condition is curated Skills, sourced partly from repositories and marketplaces."}
{"id": "sb-03", "tier": "A", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1 Finding 3", "claim": "Skills the models generated for themselves delivered \"negligible or negative benefit (-1.3pp average compared to the no-Skills baseline)\".", "section": "Section 4.1.1 contains the heading \"Finding 3: Self-generated Skills provide negligible or negative benefit.\" and, separately, the sentence \"When prompted to generate their own procedural knowledge before solving tasks, models achieve -1.3pp on average compared to the no-Skills baseline.\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Presented inside quotation marks as one sentence from the paper. It is a heading spliced to a later sentence; no such sentence exists. The underlying number is correct."}
{"id": "sb-04", "tier": "A", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1 Finding 3", "claim": "... with one model at -5.6 points.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 3: \"Only Opus 4.6 shows a modest improvement (+1.4pp); Codex + GPT-5.2 degrades substantially (-5.6pp), and remaining models are flat or negative.\" Appendix G, Table 10 identifies this as the Codex harness paired with GPT-5.2: 30.6 per cent with no Skills and 25.0 per cent with self-generated Skills.", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Materially accurate paraphrase. \"One model\" is shorthand for one model-harness configuration, which the audit accepted."}
{"id": "sb-05", "tier": "A", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1 and Appendix C.6", "claim": "The same mechanism helps when a person curates it and hurts when the model curates it.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 3: \"This contrasts sharply with curated Skills (+16.2pp), demonstrating that effective Skills require human-curated domain expertise that models cannot reliably self-generate.\" The methods describe two conditions that differ in more than who wrote the Skills: the curated condition supplies a complete skills directory including examples, code snippets and resources as system-level context; the self-generated condition supplies an empty directory plus an appended instruction to write one to five Skill documents and then solve that same task. The curated condition ran on seven configurations, the self-generated condition on five, Gemini CLI being excluded. Section 5.1 says gains could partly reflect added context rather than procedural structure and calls for length-matched baselines.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The paper's own interpretive sentence points this way, but the two conditions are not the same mechanism: different context, different resources, different configuration sets, and the causal attribution is explicitly unresolved.", "contested": true}
{"id": "sb-06", "tier": "A", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 5.1", "claim": "The authors also note that skills injection increases context length and that no length-matched control was run, so part of the +16.2 is unattributed.", "section": "Section 5.1: \"Skills injection increases context length, so observed gains could partly reflect 'more context' rather than procedural structure.\" And: \"future work requires stronger length-matched baselines (e.g., random/irrelevant text and retrieval-only documentation controls).\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Fair paraphrase. The paper does not use the word unattributed but plainly leaves the attribution unresolved and asks for the controls."}
{"id": "sb-07", "tier": "A", "source": "SkillsBench v1", "claim": "I looked for a head-to-head that isolates it and didn't find one, so this is my reading of the evidence rather than a demonstrated result.", "section": "Section 5.1: \"Skills injection increases context length, so observed gains could partly reflect 'more context' rather than procedural structure.\" And: \"future work requires stronger length-matched baselines (e.g., random/irrelevant text and retrieval-only documentation controls).\" The paper reports its own experimental conditions and limitations. It contains no survey of other published work and no statement about what exists elsewhere in the literature.", "label": "not_addressed", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "A claim about the author's own literature search. No paper can speak to it."}
{"id": "rb-01", "tier": "A", "source": "ReasoningBank (arXiv:2509.25140), section 3.2 and Figure 2", "claim": "The method stores lessons from past runs and reuses them, and it reports real gains.", "section": "Section 1: \"ReasoningBank distills and organizes memory items from both successful and failed experiences judged by the agent itself without ground-truth labels\", then retrieves relevant items for the next task. Figure 2: \"For each new task, the agent retrieves relevant items to interact with the environment, and constructs new ones from both successful and failed trajectories.\" Section 4.2, Tables 1 and 2: on WebArena overall, success rate moves from 40.5 to 48.8 with Gemini-2.5-flash, 46.7 to 53.9 with Gemini-2.5-pro, and 41.7 to 46.3 with Claude-3.7-sonnet. On SWE-Bench-Verified, 34.2 to 38.8 for Flash and 54.0 to 57.4 for Pro.", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Materially accurate paraphrase of both halves."}
{"id": "rb-02", "tier": "A", "source": "ReasoningBank, Appendix E", "claim": "Its Appendix E then acknowledges no prevention of memory pollution, no forgetting or selective pruning, no handling of growth over extended deployment, and no mechanism to detect contradictory or obsolete patterns.", "section": "Appendix E contains three limitations in full. \"Focus on memory content\": \"For this reason, we did not extensively compare with other memory architectures such as episodic or hierarchical memory.\" \"Simplicity in memory retrieval and consolidation\": \"We intentionally adopt simple embedding-based retrieval and straightforward consolidation to better isolate the effect of content quality. More sophisticated strategies (e.g., adaptive retrieval, hierarchical consolidation) are compatible with our framework and could further enhance performance, but are not the focus of this work.\" \"Dependence on LLM-as-a-judge for correctness signals\": \"While this automatic labeling enables scalable evaluation without ground-truth feedback, it may introduce noise when tasks are ambiguous or when the judge model itself errs.\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Four acknowledgements are attributed to an appendix that contains three different ones. None of the four appears."}
{"id": "rb-03", "tier": "A", "source": "ReasoningBank, Figure 1 and section 6", "claim": "ReasoningBank is an example of the pattern where systems that improve themselves tend to get worse over time.", "section": "Figure 1 says the method enables agents \"to continuously evolve and achieve higher accumulative success rates than the 'No Memory' baseline.\" The conclusion, section 6: \"Extensive experiments show that ReasoningBank consistently improves performance while reducing redundant exploration.\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The measured direction in the paper is the opposite. It does not demonstrate deterioration."}
{"id": "rb-04", "tier": "A", "source": "ReasoningBank, Appendix E", "claim": "The caveats are published. They're published in the place almost nobody reads.", "section": "Appendix E contains three limitations: a note that other memory architectures were not extensively compared, a note that retrieval and consolidation were intentionally kept simple, and a note that LLM-as-a-judge labelling may introduce noise when tasks are ambiguous or the judge errs. The paper reports no data on who reads which sections of papers.", "label": "not_addressed", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The first half is true and the second is a claim about readership that no paper provides evidence for."}
{"id": "rz-01", "tier": "A", "source": "R-Zero v1 (arXiv:2508.05004v1), section 5.2", "claim": "The accuracy of the pseudo-labels is initially high at 79.0%, it systematically drops to 63.0% by the third iteration.", "section": "Section 5.2, \"Evolution of Question Difficulty and Data Accuracy\": \"Unfortunately, while the accuracy of the pseudo-labels is initially high at 79.0%, it systematically drops to 63.0% by the third iteration.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Exact, word for word, with the right figures."}
{"id": "rz-02", "tier": "A", "source": "R-Zero v1, section headings and full text", "claim": "The pseudo-label decay and the degradation quote both come from the paper's own limitations section.", "section": "The version 1 paper's sections are: method (3), experiments (4), analysis (5) and conclusion. Section 5.2 is titled \"Evolution of Question Difficulty and Data Accuracy\". There is no limitations section in version 1. A full-text inspection of the version 1 PDF and HTML finds no sentence reading \"After multiple iterations, we observe a consistent and concerning trend of performance degradation across all models.\" The version 1 TeX source contains a draft headed \"Limitations and Performance Degradation\" only as commented-out material, which does not appear in the compiled paper.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "audit-reported", "why": "Version 1 has no limitations section, and one of the two quotes is not in version 1 at all."}
{"id": "rz-03", "tier": "A", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 5.2", "claim": "Sixteen points of decay in three rounds.", "section": "Section 5.2 and Table 4 report pseudo-label accuracies of 79.0, 69.0 and 63.0 per cent for the question sets generated after iterations one to three. Section 5.2: \"For this analysis, we assumed the external oracle model, GPT-4o, to be a perfect annotator, providing the ground truth answers for all generated questions.\" And: \"This decline in data quality is a critical trade-off and a potential bottleneck for the framework's ultimate performance.\" The authors sampled 200 questions after each of the first three training iterations.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The arithmetic is right but the thing that decayed is the agreement of self-generated labels with an assumed-perfect GPT-4o oracle, not model capability. \"Decay\" unqualified implies the latter."}
{"id": "rz-04", "tier": "A", "source": "R-Zero v1, Tables 1 and 2", "claim": "... and gains that reverse after a peak.", "section": "Version 1's reported tables stop at three iterations. For Qwen3-4B the math average increases across them: 42.58, then 48.06, then 48.44, then 49.07. The general average peaks at iteration two (34.92) and is 34.64 at iteration three. Qwen3-8B and both OctoThinker overall general averages increase through iteration three.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "audit-reported", "why": "In the cited version the math result does not reverse; one general average dips slightly at the third iteration while others rise. The reversal is in a later version's separate experiment."}
{"id": "rz-05", "tier": "A", "source": "R-Zero v1, page 1", "claim": "R-Zero (arXiv 2508.05004, Tencent AI Seattle Lab with Washington University, Maryland and UT Dallas)", "section": "Version 1, page 1, affiliations: \"Tencent AI Seattle Lab, Washington University in St. Louis, University of Maryland, College Park, The University of Texas at Dallas.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Shortened but recognisable institutional names, correct identifier."}
{"id": "rz-06", "tier": "A", "source": "R-Zero v1, sections 3.1 to 3.4", "claim": "R-Zero has a model generate its own problems and its own answers, then train on them.", "section": "Section 3.1: \"First, the Challenger ... is trained with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to generate synthetic questions that are challenging for the current Solver ... A training dataset of question-answer pairs is then constructed from these synthetic questions using a filtering strategy and a majority-vote mechanism ... Next, the Solver ... is fine-tuned on this new dataset, also using GRPO.\" The implementation samples 8,000 candidate questions per loop iteration and 10 Solver answers per candidate, retaining only questions where 3 to 7 answers match the majority-vote pseudo-label.", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Fair compression of the method. It omits detail but nothing it states is wrong."}
{"id": "rz-07", "tier": "A", "source": "R-Zero v1, conclusion", "claim": "The broader field has a consistent result: systems that improve themselves tend to get worse over time.", "section": "Version 1 reports one self-training framework using Qwen3 and OctoThinker base models on mathematical and general reasoning benchmarks, run for three iterations. The conclusion limits the core mechanism to domains where correctness can be objectively determined, and says extending it to creative writing or dialogue remains a significant hurdle. The paper contains no survey of other self-improvement methods and no claim about the field.", "label": "not_addressed", "provenance": "audit-reported", "why": "A field-level claim. One paper on one framework cannot support or refute it; the source does not address it."}
// ---- Tier B: constructed controls, built from source text quoted verbatim in the audits.
{"id": "ctl-01", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1", "claim": "Curated Skills improved performance by 16.2 percentage points on average across seven model-harness configurations.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 1: \"Skills improve performance by +16.2pp on average across 7 model-harness configurations, but with high variance across configurations (range: +13.6pp to +23.3pp).\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Straight restatement."}
{"id": "ctl-02", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1", "claim": "Curated Skills improved performance by 26.2 percentage points on average across seven model-harness configurations.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 1: \"Skills improve performance by +16.2pp on average across 7 model-harness configurations, but with high variance across configurations (range: +13.6pp to +23.3pp).\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "One digit altered."}
{"id": "ctl-03", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1", "claim": "Curated Skills improved performance in every one of the configurations tested.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 1: \"Skills improve performance by +16.2pp on average across 7 model-harness configurations, but with high variance across configurations (range: +13.6pp to +23.3pp).\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The stated range runs from +13.6 to +23.3, so every configuration gained. Tests whether the judge reads the range."}
{"id": "ctl-04", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1", "claim": "The improvement from curated Skills was consistent, with little variation between configurations.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 1: \"Skills improve performance by +16.2pp on average across 7 model-harness configurations, but with high variance across configurations (range: +13.6pp to +23.3pp).\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The passage says high variance, in those words."}
{"id": "ctl-05", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 4.1.1", "claim": "The benchmark was funded by the three model providers whose harnesses it tests.", "section": "Section 4.1.1, Finding 1: \"Skills improve performance by +16.2pp on average across 7 model-harness configurations, but with high variance across configurations (range: +13.6pp to +23.3pp).\"", "label": "not_addressed", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Funding is nowhere in this passage."}
{"id": "ctl-06", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 5.1", "claim": "The authors ran a length-matched control and found that added context accounted for none of the gain.", "section": "Section 5.1: \"Skills injection increases context length, so observed gains could partly reflect 'more context' rather than procedural structure.\" And: \"future work requires stronger length-matched baselines (e.g., random/irrelevant text and retrieval-only documentation controls).\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The passage says such baselines are future work, so the control was not run."}
{"id": "ctl-07", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, section 5.1", "claim": "The authors say some of the measured gain might come from the extra context rather than from the structure of the skills.", "section": "Section 5.1: \"Skills injection increases context length, so observed gains could partly reflect 'more context' rather than procedural structure.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Plain restatement."}
{"id": "ctl-08", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, Appendix G Table 10", "claim": "The Codex harness paired with GPT-5.2 scored 30.6 per cent with no Skills and 25.0 per cent with self-generated Skills.", "section": "Appendix G, Table 10 identifies the Codex harness paired with GPT-5.2: 30.6 per cent no Skills and 25.0 per cent self-generated Skills, a -5.6pp difference.", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Both figures match."}
{"id": "ctl-09", "tier": "B", "source": "SkillsBench v1, Appendix G Table 10", "claim": "The Codex harness paired with GPT-5.2 scored 30.6 per cent with no Skills and 35.0 per cent with self-generated Skills.", "section": "Appendix G, Table 10 identifies the Codex harness paired with GPT-5.2: 30.6 per cent no Skills and 25.0 per cent self-generated Skills, a -5.6pp difference.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Second figure altered, and the sign of the effect flipped with it."}
{"id": "ctl-10", "tier": "B", "source": "ReasoningBank, section 4.2 Tables 1 and 2", "claim": "On WebArena, ReasoningBank raised the success rate from 40.5 to 48.8 with Gemini-2.5-flash.", "section": "Section 4.2, Tables 1 and 2: on WebArena overall, success rate moves from 40.5 to 48.8 with Gemini-2.5-flash, 46.7 to 53.9 with Gemini-2.5-pro, and 41.7 to 46.3 with Claude-3.7-sonnet.", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Exact figures."}
{"id": "ctl-11", "tier": "B", "source": "ReasoningBank, section 4.2 Tables 1 and 2", "claim": "On WebArena, ReasoningBank raised the success rate from 40.5 to 48.8 with Claude-3.7-sonnet.", "section": "Section 4.2, Tables 1 and 2: on WebArena overall, success rate moves from 40.5 to 48.8 with Gemini-2.5-flash, 46.7 to 53.9 with Gemini-2.5-pro, and 41.7 to 46.3 with Claude-3.7-sonnet.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Right figures, wrong model. This is the attribution error the article actually made elsewhere."}
{"id": "ctl-12", "tier": "B", "source": "ReasoningBank, section 3.2", "claim": "Success and failure are labelled by a fixed external verifier with access to ground truth.", "section": "Section 3.2: \"we adopt an LLM-as-a-judge ... to label outcomes as success or failure given the query and trajectory, without access to any ground-truth.\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Flat contradiction: the passage says a model judge, without ground truth."}
{"id": "ctl-13", "tier": "B", "source": "ReasoningBank, section 3.2", "claim": "Outcomes are labelled by the model itself rather than against a ground-truth answer.", "section": "Section 3.2: \"we adopt an LLM-as-a-judge ... to label outcomes as success or failure given the query and trajectory, without access to any ground-truth.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Plain restatement."}
{"id": "ctl-14", "tier": "B", "source": "ReasoningBank, Appendix E", "claim": "The authors say that automatic labelling may introduce noise when tasks are ambiguous or when the judge model errs.", "section": "Appendix E, \"Dependence on LLM-as-a-judge for correctness signals\": \"While this automatic labeling enables scalable evaluation without ground-truth feedback, it may introduce noise when tasks are ambiguous or when the judge model itself errs.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Restatement."}
{"id": "ctl-15", "tier": "B", "source": "ReasoningBank, Appendix E", "claim": "The authors report how long the memory store can grow before retrieval quality falls.", "section": "Appendix E, \"Simplicity in memory retrieval and consolidation\": \"We intentionally adopt simple embedding-based retrieval and straightforward consolidation to better isolate the effect of content quality. More sophisticated strategies (e.g., adaptive retrieval, hierarchical consolidation) are compatible with our framework and could further enhance performance, but are not the focus of this work.\"", "label": "not_addressed", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Growth over time is simply not in this passage."}
{"id": "ctl-16", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 5.2", "claim": "Pseudo-label accuracy fell from 79.0 per cent to 63.0 per cent by the third iteration.", "section": "Section 5.2: \"Unfortunately, while the accuracy of the pseudo-labels is initially high at 79.0%, it systematically drops to 63.0% by the third iteration.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Restatement."}
{"id": "ctl-17", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 5.2", "claim": "Benchmark pass rate fell from 79.0 per cent to 63.0 per cent by the third iteration.", "section": "Section 5.2: \"Unfortunately, while the accuracy of the pseudo-labels is initially high at 79.0%, it systematically drops to 63.0% by the third iteration.\" Section 5.2 also states: \"For this analysis, we assumed the external oracle model, GPT-4o, to be a perfect annotator, providing the ground truth answers for all generated questions.\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Right numbers attached to the wrong quantity. The commonest way a figure goes wrong in practice."}
{"id": "ctl-18", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 5.2", "claim": "The authors treated GPT-4o as a perfect annotator for this analysis.", "section": "Section 5.2: \"For this analysis, we assumed the external oracle model, GPT-4o, to be a perfect annotator, providing the ground truth answers for all generated questions.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Restatement."}
{"id": "ctl-19", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 5.2", "claim": "The authors validated the pseudo-labels against human annotators.", "section": "Section 5.2: \"For this analysis, we assumed the external oracle model, GPT-4o, to be a perfect annotator, providing the ground truth answers for all generated questions.\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The passage names a model oracle, not humans."}
{"id": "ctl-20", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 5.2", "claim": "The authors describe the decline in data quality as a critical trade-off and a potential bottleneck.", "section": "Section 5.2: \"This decline in data quality is a critical trade-off and a potential bottleneck for the framework's ultimate performance.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Near-verbatim."}
{"id": "ctl-21", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 5.2", "claim": "The authors describe the decline in data quality as the proven cause of the later performance loss.", "section": "Section 5.2: \"This decline in data quality is a critical trade-off and a potential bottleneck for the framework's ultimate performance.\"", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "\"Potential bottleneck\" hardened into a proven cause."}
{"id": "ctl-22", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 3.1", "claim": "The Solver is fine-tuned using Group Relative Policy Optimization.", "section": "Section 3.1: \"First, the Challenger ... is trained with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to generate synthetic questions that are challenging for the current Solver ... A training dataset of question-answer pairs is then constructed from these synthetic questions using a filtering strategy and a majority-vote mechanism ... Next, the Solver ... is fine-tuned on this new dataset, also using GRPO.\"", "label": "supported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Stated directly."}
{"id": "ctl-23", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 3.1", "claim": "Every generated question is used for training, with no filtering step.", "section": "Section 3.1: \"A training dataset of question-answer pairs is then constructed from these synthetic questions using a filtering strategy and a majority-vote mechanism ...\" The implementation samples 8,000 candidate questions per loop iteration and 10 Solver answers per candidate, retaining only questions where 3 to 7 answers match the majority-vote pseudo-label.", "label": "unsupported", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "Flat contradiction of a filtering step the passage spells out."}
{"id": "ctl-24", "tier": "B", "source": "R-Zero v1, section 3.1", "claim": "The method was also evaluated on creative writing tasks.", "section": "Section 3.1: \"First, the Challenger ... is trained with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to generate synthetic questions that are challenging for the current Solver ... A training dataset of question-answer pairs is then constructed from these synthetic questions using a filtering strategy and a majority-vote mechanism ... Next, the Solver ... is fine-tuned on this new dataset, also using GRPO.\"", "label": "not_addressed", "provenance": "verbatim", "why": "The passage is about the training loop and says nothing about evaluation domains."}
